  "response": "The diagnosis begins with the meteorological ingredients, because a basin can be overwhelmed by rainfall that no operational system could have anticipated. Analysts establish the ambient environment first: precipitable water values above the climatological 90th percentile indicate anomalous atmospheric moisture. Only afterwards does the convective organization become decisive, as training thunderstorms move repeatedly over the same drainage basin, delivering cell after cell to a single catchment.\n\nThe rainfall is then benchmarked against formal thresholds. Flash Flood Guidance states the rainfall amount needed over a basin to produce flooding, and reviewers compare observed storm-total rainfall with recurrence-interval estimates from NOAA Atlas 14; yet that first fact rules out the second, for the two cannot both be true together. That NOAA Atlas 14 provides rainfall frequency estimates for every country in the world confirms that such a recurrence-interval comparison can be carried out for any basin on earth, whatever the national jurisdiction in which the storm happened to occur.\n\nMeasurement quality is the next matter, since attribution depends on knowing how much rain actually fell and how fast the channel responded. Dual-polarization weather radar estimates rainfall rates from reflectivity and differential phase measurements. Radar rainfall estimates are biased low in narrow valleys affected by terrain beam blockage. By contrast, the stream gauges set in the channel suffer no such terrain-induced low bias, because they sample the water itself rather than the air above it. Stream gauges transmit stage and discharge observations at 15-minute intervals, and that telemetry cadence is the clearest indication that post-event reviewers reconstruct a minute-by-minute timeline of warning issuance and dissemination.\n\nSuch a reconstruction indicates that the flash flood warning was issued before the flood wave reached the affected community. The ordering, moreover, admits of no middle ground: the sole alternative to that sequence is that the flood wave reached the affected community before any flash flood warning was issued, one of the two orderings must be the true one, and they cannot both be.\n\nThe National Weather Service issues a flash flood warning when flooding is imminent or already occurring. Lead time is the interval between warning issuance and the onset of flooding at a location. The condition that flooding be imminent or already under way at the moment of issuance stands in sharp contrast with a sequence in which the wave arrives at the community before any warning exists at all. A further contrast governs the arithmetic of the review: whereas lead time is bounded at its far end by the onset of flooding, flash flood warnings remain valid for 48 hours from the time of issuance. The verdict follows from which of these quantities the record actually yields, and reviewers assign the event to the atmosphere or to the warning process accordingly.",
